 ## Signing for the same address
 
-The previous model identified an account by its wallet address. A wallet signature served as the sign-in credential, and the private key stayed in the original wallet. The new system uses an email or social identity for sign-in, with Privy providing the wallet attached to that account. Signing a message with Sign in with Solana (SIWS) proves that someone controls an address at the time. A platform can use it to link the old profile. Continued use of an external wallet still requires the wallet or a separately authorized signer to approve each transaction.
+The previous model identified an account by its wallet address. A wallet signature served as the sign-in credential, and the private key stayed in the original wallet. The new system uses an email or social identity for sign-in, with Privy providing the wallet attached to that account. A message signed through Sign in with Solana (SIWS) proves control of the address at that moment. That gives the platform a way to link the old profile. Continued use of an external wallet still requires the wallet or a separately authorized signer to approve each transaction.
 
-To produce signatures directly for the same ordinary Solana keypair address, Privy needs the same signing key. Key import solves that particular product requirement. A verifiable signature could link the existing account, and scoped, revocable delegation could cover certain actions. The permissions granted by these options differ. [Solana documentation on signing and authentication](https://solana.com/docs/frontend/messages-and-auth) · [Privy wallet policies and controls](https://docs.privy.io/security/wallet-infrastructure/policy-and-controls)
