 Privy's public documentation describes Hybrid Public Key Encryption (HPKE) with a temporary public key. The private key material is decrypted in a trusted execution environment (TEE) and used to initialize the wallet. The temporary keypair is then destroyed. The encrypted transport runs from the browser to Privy's TEE. Former Mt. Gox CEO Mark Karpelès argued that calling this end-to-end encryption can mislead users who take it to mean only they can use the key. [Privy import architecture](https://docs.privy.io/wallets/wallets/import-a-wallet/architecture) · [Coverage of Karpelès's criticism](https://tech.yahoo.com/cybersecurity/articles/pump-fun-faces-pushback-asking-213700831.html)
 
