 ## The cost to users
 
-Karpelès pointed to two issues. Users first have to trust the import interface they give the key to. They then have to trust the identity of the encryption recipient and the isolation of Privy's TEE. Platform access depends on the email or social identity as well as the wallet's authorization settings. Account takeover, a ban, a court order, or compliance restrictions can affect signing and recovery within the platform. Users who retain the original key still have an independent way to use the old address. Closing a platform account does not rotate the key on-chain. The public materials do not establish how long the provider retains it after deletion. SIWS can link a profile while keeping an external wallet self-custodial. The platform gets different permissions with a key import. [Coverage of Karpelès's criticism](https://tech.yahoo.com/cybersecurity/articles/pump-fun-faces-pushback-asking-213700831.html) · [Privy wallet policies and controls](https://docs.privy.io/security/wallet-infrastructure/policy-and-controls)
